# Protocol 023 — problem log

One JSON object per line. Fields follow the Protocol 019/020/021/022 convention:
`id`, `date`, `stage`, `severity`, `status`, `summary`, `detail`, `fix`/`what_this_does_not_show`, `evidence`.

A problem is written here when it changed a registered artifact, a gate result,
or the reading of one — including the problems that were found by an automated
check rather than by a human, and including the ones whose fix is "the plan's
wording had to be narrowed".

{"id": "P23-01", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S1", "severity": "high", "status": "fixed", "summary": "the compute-first regime was not byte-identical to Protocol 022 until four separate divergences were removed", "detail": "The round's whole A-arm rests on 'compute-first IS cascade_v2'.  The first draft of simulator/workload/BitbrainWorkloadProtocol023.py diverged from Protocol021CascadeBWGD2._apply_cascade in four independent ways, each of which was invisible in the aggregate metrics and only surfaced because the check demanded exact equality (verify_ftmoe_protocol023_generator.py check A-01): (1) the generator used a new ram_ramp_peak_mult of 2.5 for every regime, so A's RAM target was raw*mult*2.5 instead of P22's raw*mult*2.0, and the task that saturates the band diverged by 1078.7; (2) the RAM target was built from the *clipped* familiar base (min(raw*mult, ram_upper)) instead of P22's unclipped raw demand, which caps any task whose raw demand exceeds ram_upper; (3) the phase-shape law was a step-then-linear-release ('plateau') where P22 uses _trapezoid for RAM and disk too, so the whole interior of every RAM window was wrong; (4) the mechanism seed for compute_first was set to the new family seed 23023 instead of P22's registered 22022, which changed the draw for every creation_id while leaving the *count* of events identical, so the event-count comparison passed while every trajectory differed.", "fix": "Compute-first now carries P22's registered seed (22022) and P22's ramp multiplier (2.0); the RAM target is built from the unclipped raw demand; and _phase_shape reproduces P22's own sampling (fraction = k/(duration-1) through _trapezoid, rise=0.25) for the inherited regime.  A-01 now reports zero trajectory differences across cpu, ram and disk for every task, and the event ids and envelopes match element-wise.", "what_this_does_not_show": "Byte-identity with P22 is verified on seven tasks at one replay seed (700) and one mechanism seed, not on a collected stream; the collected dev stream is the first place the full cross-check runs at scale.", "evidence": ["verify_ftmoe_protocol023_generator.py", "test_ftmoe_protocol023_regimes.py"]}

{"id": "P23-02", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S1", "severity": "high", "status": "fixed", "summary": "the shared family parameter dict silently overwrote per-regime phase durations, which made the memory-first chain longer than the observable history", "detail": "REGIMES_V3 stores one family dict plus per-regime overrides, and regime_params() merged them.  Two different merge rules each produced a silent wrong answer: overlaying every family key replaced memory_first's ram_duration [4,5] with the family default [6,10], so the B chain reached age 12 and _validate_family rejected the family outright (longest chain age 12 >= observable_history 12); overlaying only the keys the regime declared itself, on the other hand, made a deliberately drifted cpu_burst_upper invisible to assert_registered_physics, because the transform read the live dict while the guard read the pristine registry copy.  The second failure mode is the dangerous one: a physics drift check that silently cannot fail.", "fix": "regime_params() overlays exactly the keys in _SHARED_FAMILY_KEYS (the capacity constants, the observable history and the forbidden-input list) and never the per-regime duration bands or the ramp multiplier; mechanism_seed is always taken from REGIME_MECHANISM_SEEDS.  test_drifted_physics_is_detected now drifts three different shared numerics and requires all three to be detected, and test_the_generator_records_the_chain_geometry pins the per-regime chain ages.", "evidence": ["simulator/workload/BitbrainWorkloadProtocol023.py", "test_ftmoe_protocol023_regimes.py"]}

{"id": "P23-03", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S1", "severity": "medium", "status": "fixed", "summary": "the unit-test harness reported a zero retained-disk term because its sampler never advanced the container age", "detail": "The P23 unit tests exercise the disk model without a container, so they install a stand-in container and read disk() directly.  The sampler called disk() twenty times while leaving env.interval at 0, so every task appeared to have constant disk occupancy and the retained term looked absent.  The generator was innocent: the same model read through a hand-written loop that advances the age produced the registered 17800/21000 values.  A second, equally misleading reading came from the tuned disk law itself: initial state 0 (occupancy 0) has probability 0.42, and a task that stays at 0 for its whole early life makes the retained term invisible through no fault of the mechanism.", "fix": "disk_trajectory() advances the age axis explicitly; the assertion additionally prefers a cascade task whose own baseline occupancy is non-zero and says so in a comment, because 'the mechanism is invisible for a task that has no disk occupancy to begin with' is a property of the sample, not of the regime.", "evidence": ["test_ftmoe_protocol023_regimes.py"]}

{"id": "P23-04", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S1", "severity": "medium", "status": "fixed", "summary": "two generator audit accessors were broken for every regime and the collector worked around them instead of failing", "detail": "assert_registered_physics() raised TypeError on the dict-valued response_windows (the guard fell through to float() for a dict), and task_cascade_windows() raised KeyError 'cpu_floor' for every compute-first envelope because the CPU phase's floor is registered under the inherited name cpu_burst_floor.  The S2 collector detected both at smoke time and replaced them with collector-side equivalents, documenting the substitution in manifest['registration'], which kept the collection running but would have left two public accessors permanently dead while the audit claimed to have used them.", "fix": "The guard now type-dispatches on dict values, and task_cascade_windows() resolves the CPU floor through its registered name.  A regression test covers all three regimes' task_cascade_windows output (floor, peak, window, threshold ordering).", "what_this_does_not_show": "The collected stream's manifest still records the collector-side substitution; that field is kept as written because the stream was collected while the workaround was live, and the verifier recomputes the same quantities independently.", "evidence": ["simulator/workload/BitbrainWorkloadProtocol023.py", "test_ftmoe_protocol023_regimes.py", "verify_ftmoe_protocol023_stream.py"]}

{"id": "P23-05", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S2", "severity": "high", "status": "open", "summary": "RAM and disk onset thresholds are provisional and the disk one has the narrowest margin", "detail": "The registered onset threshold for a regime must sit strictly above the measured familiar per-task maximum of its resource and strictly below the regime's own floor.  Measured on a collected stream: CPU 2600 sits between a familiar maximum of 1860 and a floor of 4400 (margin 740 / 1800); RAM 2810 sits between 1400 and 4500 (margin 1410 / 1690); disk 11600 sits between a measured familiar maximum of 6000 and a retention peak of 16000 (margin 5600 / 4400).  All three are admissible on the measured data, but the RAM and disk values were registered before any stream existed and are flagged provisional in the instrument, and the disk threshold is the one a reviewer should challenge first: the familiar disk law can reach 9000 in principle even though this stream's familiar phase only reached 6000, so a longer familiar phase could narrow that margin.", "what_would_settle_it": "re-measuring the familiar per-task disk maximum on the confirmation seeds (701+) and, if it exceeds 11600, re-registering the disk threshold before any confirmation analysis — never after.", "evidence": ["artifacts/ftmoe_online/protocol_023/development_streams/*/unseen_data_audit.json", "verify_ftmoe_protocol023_stream.py"]}

{"id": "P23-06", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S2", "severity": "medium", "status": "reported", "summary": "one regime's envelope crosses the other two regimes' onset thresholds, so an unmapped scan inflates every regime", "detail": "All three regimes move the same three resources, so a compute-first cascade (RAM ramp to 4500-6000, disk retention to 16000) also crosses the memory-first RAM threshold (2810) and the io-first disk threshold (11600).  Measured on a single-regime compute-first stream: an unmapped resource+tau scan reports 32 A onsets, 17 B onsets and 9 C onsets, while the regime-restricted task cohort reports 32 A onsets and no B or C onsets at all.  The instrument's own tests pin this, and the plan's event-count requirement ('independent fault events >= 80 per regime') would be inflated by roughly 50% if the unmapped scan were used as the gate input.", "fix": "Every gate-relevant onset scan uses the per-regime task cohort (task_regime_map), and the cross-hit count is reported next to it rather than dropped, so the confound stays visible.", "evidence": ["verify_ftmoe_protocol023_stream.py", "ftmoe_protocol023_core.py", "test_ftmoe_protocol023_core.py"]}
{"id": "P23-07", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S2", "severity": "high", "status": "open (definitional decision required)", "summary": "plan section 9's 'valid onset/follow-up >= 50 per regime' fails under the strict reading (24/36/31) and passes under the P22-compatible reading (185/200/235)", "detail": "The plan does not define 'valid'. The analyzer gates the strict reading -- an onset whose task observes every interval of the registered response window -- and measured 24 (compute-first), 36 (memory-first), 31 (io-first) against the >=50 threshold, so the registered Data Gate FAILS on exactly that one check while the other eight pass: prevalence 6.80/3.05/3.79 percent inside 3-12, independent fault events 410/260/271 >= 80, deployment rejection 13.20/8.45/9.32 percent <= 25, migration rejection 36.29/15.43/22.17 percent <= 40, worst event share 1.57/1.56/1.41 percent < 10, prevalence spread 3.75 pp <= 4 pp. The cause is structural rather than a collector defect: median observed cohort life is 5-6 intervals while the registered response horizons are 16-18 intervals, so a strict follow-up window cannot be observed for most tasks in this simulator. Protocol 022's own definition (at least one observed interval inside a window) gives 185/200/235 on the same streams.", "what_was_not_done": "The analyzer did not switch definitions after seeing the number: it reports both counts, carries a machine-readable followup block, raises WARN-FOLLOWUP-DEFINITION for each regime and exits non-zero (STOP-DATA).", "options": ["accept the strict reading, record H1 FAIL and stop before the method comparison", "amend the registered definition to the P22-compatible one, recorded as an amendment with the old value kept (the precedent is the 2.5 GiB RAM guard amendment in P22-17/19)", "shorten the registered response horizons, which changes the regimes and invalidates the S1 byte-identity result"], "recommendation": "option 2 (amendment, old value kept): it neither rewrites a measured result nor changes the scenario, and the strict reading is unreachable by construction in this simulator", "evidence": ["artifacts/ftmoe_online/protocol_023/data_audit/DATA_GATE.md", "artifacts/ftmoe_online/protocol_023/data_audit/regime_A.json", "artifacts/ftmoe_online/protocol_023/data_audit/marginal_match.json"]}
{"id": "P23-08", "date": "2026-09-12", "stage": "S3", "severity": "high", "status": "open (blocked on an artifact this round did not produce)", "summary": "the H3 gradient-interference gate cannot be evaluated at the registered start state because the residual bank is untrained", "detail": "The frozen start checkpoint carries no learner weights, and Protocol 020 R1 constructs a fresh residual bank whose final read-out layer is zeroed (recovery/PreGANSrc/src/ftmoe_online_r1.py:80-84). Measured: correction_logits.abs().max() == 0.0, only 10 of 26 trainable tensors receive any gradient, and all 8 hidden-layer parameter groups have exactly zero gradient, so the plan section 14 sub-reports router-gradient cosine and per-layer cosine have no defined value there. The probe reports those groups as null, counts them under n_pairs_undefined, excludes them from the verdict and writes INADMISSIBLE with the registered outcome preserved underneath. On the one non-degenerate block (the read-out head) all three regime pairs are strongly co-directional: mean cosines +0.860, +0.766, +0.929 and negative-pair fractions 6.3, 3.7, 4.9 percent, which under the registered rule reads STOP-GI rather than a conflict. No mature fixed-C learner checkpoint exists in this checkout and none was produced because D was out of scope.", "what_would_settle_it": "run the fixed-C arm (not D) online on the collected dev stream long enough for the residual to mature, save the learner.* tensors, and re-run the probe with --learner-state; the instrument already supports and tests that path", "why_it_matters": "H3 is the gate that most directly argues for or against dynamic experts, and the start-state reading points the opposite way from H2: the probe says the three mechanisms need different knowledge, while the untrained gradient geometry says learning them does not conflict", "evidence": ["artifacts/ftmoe_online/protocol_023/specialization/gradient_interference.json", "probe_ftmoe_protocol023_gradient.py"]}
